course: dictionary
lesson: 13011
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. Extract can be a doing word (a verb) or a naming word (a noun). The clues in the sentence show you which meaning is needed. «The dentist had to extract the bad tooth.» Here extract means to pull something out, the way a tooth is taken out.
explain: «Mum added a drop of vanilla extract to the cake mix.» Vanilla extract is a strong liquid taken from a plant, and it gives the cake its flavour. Ask yourself: what is the sentence about? A tooth, a cake, a book? That question tells you which meaning to use.
explain: «Our teacher read us an extract from a famous adventure story.» Here an extract is a short piece taken from a book. Notice the pattern: when extract is a verb, someone is doing an action, and when it is a noun, it is a thing.
fact: Extract can be a verb (extract, extracts, extracted, extracting) or a noun (an extract, extracts).
fact: As a verb, extract means to take or pull something out, often with effort.
fact: Scientists extract salt from sea water and iron from ore.
fact: As a noun, an extract is a short piece taken from a book, film or music, or a strong liquid taken from a plant or food.
fact: When a word has two meanings, try each one in the sentence and keep the one that makes sense.
exercise: «The dentist had to extract the tooth.» What does extract mean here? | A strong liquid from a plant | To glow brightly | *To pull out | A short piece of a story
exercise: «Our teacher read us an extract from a famous novel.» What is an extract? | *A short piece taken from a book | A machine for squeezing juice | To take money from someone | A tall tree
exercise: «Mum added vanilla extract to the cake.» What is vanilla extract? | A tool for cutting fruit | A kind of biscuit | *A strong liquid taken from a plant | To pull out a tooth
test: «The reporter tried to extract the secret from the spy.» What does extract mean here? | To pull out a tooth | *To get information out of someone who does not want to give it | A short piece of writing | A strong flavouring
test: In «He wants to extract the juice from the lemon», what kind of word is extract? | *Verb | Adjective | Noun | Adverb
exam: Which sentence uses extract as a noun? | «She wanted to extract the truth from him.» | «The cook will extract the juice from the fruit.» | «The miners began to extract the gold.» | *«The teacher read us an extract from the book.»
